<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heimskringla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1230</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heimskringla is a collection of sagas of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> written in Old Norse around 1230, usually attributed to the Icelandic historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>. Beginning with the legendary Yngling kings, it runs through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Saint Olaf</a> to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-erlingsson">Magnus Erlingsson</a> in 1177. It is a central, if often legendary, source for Viking-Age and medieval Scandinavian history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heimskringla.jpg" alt="Heimskringla" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National and University Library of Iceland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lbs_fragm_82,_0001r_-_0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
